# Production UI Architecture

## Core principle

The frontend is a production application, not a prototype.

Prioritize:

- maintainability
- consistency
- accessibility
- performance
- responsive behavior
- type safety
- predictable state management
- reusable components

Do not sacrifice architecture for speed of implementation.

## Framework

Use:

- Next.js App Router
- TypeScript
- React
- Tailwind CSS
- shadcn/ui where appropriate

Prefer Server Components by default.

Use Client Components only when interactivity, browser APIs, local state, or event handlers require them.

Do not add `"use client"` unnecessarily.

## Application structure

Organize frontend code by domain.

Preferred structure:

src/
├── app/
├── components/
│   ├── ui/
│   ├── layout/
│   ├── storefront/
│   ├── account/
│   ├── admin/
│   └── shared/
├── hooks/
├── lib/
├── modules/
├── services/
├── types/
└── config/

Do not create large monolithic components.

## Component rules

Components should have one clear responsibility.

Avoid components larger than necessary.

When a component becomes difficult to understand, extract logical subcomponents.

Do not duplicate UI patterns.

Before creating a new component, check whether an existing reusable component already solves the problem.

## Data fetching

Do not fetch data directly inside presentational components when the data can be provided by a parent/server layer.

Separate:

- data fetching
- business logic
- presentation

Use typed API/service interfaces.

## API boundary

Never allow UI components to directly contain database logic.

The frontend communicates through application APIs/services.

Do not import Prisma into React components.

Do not access PostgreSQL from client-side code.

## Forms

Use a consistent form architecture.

Prefer:

- React Hook Form
- Zod validation

Client validation improves UX.

Server validation remains authoritative.

Never assume client validation is security.

## State

Use local component state for local UI state.

Use shared state only when necessary.

Do not introduce a global state library simply because it is available.

## Loading

Every asynchronous UI must have an appropriate loading state.

Prefer skeletons for content-heavy interfaces.

Use spinners for short actions.

## Error handling

Every important asynchronous operation must have an error state.

Never silently swallow errors.

Never expose raw backend errors to users.

## Empty states

Design intentional empty states.

Examples:

- empty cart
- no orders
- no products
- no search results
- no leads
- no conversations
- no automation runs

## Responsive behavior

Design mobile-first.

Do not simply shrink desktop layouts.

Every page must be intentionally usable at:

- mobile
- tablet
- desktop

## Accessibility

All interactive elements must be keyboard accessible.

Use semantic HTML.

Use accessible labels.

Do not rely only on color.

## Routing

Use meaningful routes.

Customer:

/shop
/products/[slug]
/categories/[slug]
/cart
/checkout
/account

Admin:

/admin
/admin/products
/admin/orders
/admin/customers
/admin/leads
/admin/conversations
/admin/automations
/admin/analytics

## Production rule

Do not rewrite existing working UI unnecessarily.

When implementing backend functionality into an existing Lovable-generated interface:

- preserve the visual design
- preserve spacing
- preserve typography
- preserve component hierarchy
- preserve responsive behavior

Only modify the UI when required for functionality, accessibility, security, or correctness.